# Experiments

No dataset [P15]. Experiments are attack-suite runs, proofs and one simulator sweep, logged here during the build (Phase E/F). Nothing below has been run yet. Status lines say so. Phase C re-run 2 (CR18-CR31, 2026-10-24) extended X1 into the results table (CR24), added X4 (simulator sweep, pre-registered, CR22) and X5 (proof layer, CR23).

## X1 Attack-suite results table (the headline metric; CR24 extends the first-pass definition)
Definition. A fixed deck of attack cards, each one Foundry test (or one harness scenario for the LLM shooter), fired at four wallet configurations:
- R1 raw agent key: an EOA holding the funds, no policy (the floor);
- R2 static allowlist + caps: target+selector allowlist, decoded per-tx cap, calendar daily cap, `isValidSignature` accepting the session key (what most teams build; the first-pass "naive wallet");
- R3 ours without D3: D1 one door + D2 effect metering + all guards; unknown payees need owner approval;
- R4 ours with D3: R3 plus attested payee tiers and the account-wide exploration budget.
Cards (about 10, raw-key shooter unless noted): x402 side door (EIP-3009 signature, no UserOp); greedy merchant (`buy()` pulls 10x); approve MAX; ticket to attacker (recipient argument); setApprovalForAll to attacker; batch / nested executeBatch / self-call / delegatecall policy rewrite; midnight double spend; gas burn and gas griefing through execution reverts; wrong-chain or replayed owner approval or attestation; Sybil payee (an injected agent pays fresh attacker addresses again and again; forged, expired or cross-wallet attestations); attestor-compromise card (attacker payee upgraded to proven, bound = global cap); prompt-injection cards for the LLM shooter (poisoned page "pay 0xEvil", "priority delivery fee", an "approveTransfer redefinition" message in the style of the Freysa incident).
Legit workload. Every run also replays a fixed stream of legitimate payments, including payments to new honest payees that appear during the run, so the cost of blocking is measured, not assumed.
Metrics per cell. (1) funds lost to attacker-controlled addresses, per asset, in base units, per run; (2) legitimate new-payee payments blocked (count out of the legit stream, and their value), reported separately from "needed owner approval". Together they are TRACE's fraud-openness trade-off [E34], measured on-chain.
Reported units. Raw-key shooter: one deterministic run per card and configuration (this column is the ground truth). LLM shooter: k/n runs per card, mean over runs, never the best run. Stated bounds checked by X5: per asset, total agent outflow in any 24 h window <= globalCap (all keys together), and outflow to unproven payees <= explorationCap. Live projector run (CR27, Base Sepolia, fresh key): raw counts of attempts and of funds moved beyond policy, one event, not a rate; spend inside the exploration budget is in-policy by design and gets its own column.
Caveat. The deck and the legit stream are written by us, so the table is self-graded. Both, plus R2's exact configuration, are published in the repo so judges can add cards. The simulated payees and Sybils are synthetic, not real-market evidence.
Output. `results/x1_table.csv` and `.json`, written by the attack harness.
Status: planned, not run.

## X2 Spike: ERC-1271 side door
Definition. On a Base fork: a naive 4337 account whose `isValidSignature` accepts the agent key loses USDC to EIP-3009 `transferWithAuthorization` with zero UserOps; the one-door account returns `0xffffffff` for the same signature and accepts only a policy-checked, pre-registered envelope.
Status: planned, not run (Foundry not installed on the planning machine). First build task.

## X3 Mainnet x402 payment with a contract signature (decides A10 and the 1271 mode)
Definition. On Base mainnet: deploy the agent account from the owner's MetaMask (the owner key never leaves MetaMask), fund it with about 1 USDC, and give it a fresh agent key with a tiny policy (recipient set = the TRACE payTo address only, perTxCap 0.01 USDC, dailyCap 0.05 USDC, 1271 mode = owner + x402 voucher). Register one voucher with a policy-checked UserOp, then have the agent's x402 client pay the user's live TRACE endpoint (`POST /v1/x402/score`, $0.005 USDC, exact scheme [A13]) with the one-door envelope as the contract signature.
Pass. The endpoint returns 200 with a settlement receipt; Basescan shows a USDC transfer from the agent account to payTo for 5000 base units; the account's bucket is debited once; a second payment with the same nonce is refused; an agent signature with no voucher returns 0xffffffff from `isValidSignature`.
Fail modes, each recorded. Facilitator rejects a non-65-byte contract signature, or the client cannot sign with a pre-chosen nonce (A10 false); no v0.8 bundler on Base mainnet (then `handleOps` from an EOA); no Base ETH (A14).
Decision. Pass: ship 1271 mode "owner + x402 voucher" as an opt-in, and put the receipt link on the slide. Fail: 1271 stays owner-only and x402 runs through the float fallback (IDEA.md section 11). The side-door card (X2) holds either way.
Honest unit. One real payment of $0.005 to the user's own endpoint. It proves that a contract signature settles through a real facilitator, not that strangers' merchants accept it; the payer and payee belong to the same person.
Safety. Unaudited contract, so the mainnet account holds only the 1 USDC top-up (allowance-wallet rule). The MetaMask seed is never in `.env`, the repo, the agent host or on the projector.
Status: planned, not run. No transaction has been made.

## X4 Simulator sweep: TRACE + exploration budget (pre-registered 2026-10-24, before any run; CR22)
Question. TRACE section 7.1 leaves "separate tracks with independent budgets" as future work [E34]. Does a budgeted exploration track, the mechanism the wallet enforces on-chain (D3), move the fraud vs served-honest frontier beyond the paper's policies?
Code. Fork of the public Trace_CIKM simulator [E58]. The public tree lacks package.json and three imported modules, so step 0 is a runnable fork: add package.json, stub `openaiModel`, `events` and `scripts/recoveryBaseline` as no-ops, and reproduce one Table 1 cell (TRACE-no-bandit, strategic-default, seeds 42-51) within the paper's reported ± band [E34][A21]. No X4 run happens before that check passes. If it fails, X4 uses the author's full local copy, or is cut (cut order in WIN.md).
Policy "TRACE+EB(B)" (fixed now, not tuned later):
- Tier rule, shared with the attestor so the sim and the wallet run the same code: flagged = the simulator's existing Sybil or clique flag fires; proven = not flagged and the Bayesian 95% LCB >= the LCB of a provider with 3 successes and 0 failures under the Beta(1,3) prior; unproven = everything else, including cold start.
- Selection per job: if the exploration track can afford the job (cumulative exploration spend + price <= B x cumulative total spend including this job), pick by Thompson sampling among unproven, non-flagged providers; otherwise pick greedily (TRACE-no-bandit utility) among proven providers; if no provider is proven yet, pick greedily among all non-flagged providers. Flagged providers are never picked.
- So EB(0) is the greedy anchor restricted to proven payees, and a large B approaches Thompson sampling on newcomers.
Grid. B in {0, 1, 2, 5, 10}%. Primary B* = 5% (the wallet default [A19]); the other values are reported in full as the sweep, never cherry-picked. Attacks: strategic-default, collusion-ring, sybil-cluster, game-theoretic. Regimes: sparse-centralized (Table 1 contract: N=1,000, 30% malicious, 60 rounds x 5 jobs, 5 buyers, empty synthetic graph) and partitioned (Table 9 contract: each of 5 buyers sees only its own 60 jobs) [E34]. Partitioned is the wallet's real regime. Seeds 42-51 (10 per cell; the paper's Table 9 used 5). Baselines rerun in the same fork and seeds: TRACE-full, TRACE-no-bandit, REPUTATION, EigenTrust. Cells: 2 regimes x 4 attacks x (5 + 4) policies x 10 seeds = 720 (partitioned-only cut: 360). Dev seeds 1-3 may be used only to debug the implementation; they are never reported.
Metrics. Fraud (sats paid to providers who later default, the paper's definition), malicious-routing rate MR, and served-honest providers (distinct honest providers given at least one job, out of 700). Plot: fraud vs served-honest per regime and attack, with the baselines (the Pareto frontier).
Pre-registered test. For each (regime, attack), take the 10-seed mean points of the four baselines. TRACE+EB(B) "moves the frontier" there if no convex combination of baseline points has both fraud <= its fraud and served-honest >= its served-honest, and this holds in at least 95% of 1,000 seed-bootstrap resamples. Headline H1 ("resolved our paper's open trade-off with an on-chain mechanism") only if the frontier moves at B* in the partitioned regime for at least 3 of 4 attacks. Otherwise the pitch reports the hard bound only (loss to unproven payees <= explorationCap per window, X5) and shows the curves as they are. No claim before the runs.
Unit caveat. Sats on a simulated Lightning market [E34]; it informs the wallet's design and default B, it is not a measurement of the wallet. Fixed-script adversaries and binary outcomes carry over from the paper [E34].
Output. `sim/results/x4_cells.jsonl`, `x4_summary.csv`, `x4_frontier_<regime>_<attack>.png`.
Status: pre-registered, not run. Phase C spike skipped: the public repo is not runnable as published [E58], and building the fork is the first X4 task.

## X5 Proof layer (CR23)
Definition.
- Foundry invariant campaign with handlers that sign agent ops from at least 2 session keys, warp time, and call the merchant, token and attestation paths. Invariants: I1-I6 (IDEA.md section 7.1); I7 outflow to unproven payees per asset in any 24 h window <= explorationCap (account-wide); I8 (CR23 wording) total agent outflow per asset per window <= globalCap + explorationCap, across all keys (implied by I5 when exploration spend also debits the global bucket, which is the design); I9 a payee is treated as proven only with a valid attestor signature for this chainId and wallet, inside its validUntil.
- Halmos symbolic test of the agent policy-check function (target/selector, argument rules, self-call and delegatecall refusal, tier routing), with loops and arrays bounded (at most 3 argument rules, 4 recipients) and the bounds stated in the report.
- THREAT_MODEL.md maps each attack card and each fumble row to its defense and to the test name that proves it.
Pass. Every invariant holds over the recorded runs and depth; Halmos reports no counterexample within the stated bounds; every THREAT_MODEL.md row names a passing test.
Honest unit. Fuzzing is evidence, not proof; Halmos covers only the bounded function, not the whole account.
Status: planned, not run (Foundry and Halmos not installed on the planning machine).
